1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,564
Homogeneous equations are equations that only

2
00:00:03,564 --> 00:00:06,758
care about one thing: the ratio y over x.

3
00:00:07,478 --> 00:00:10,636
Here is the prototype from Lecture 5.

4
00:00:10,636 --> 00:00:15,659
dy/dx equals one, plus y over x, plus y over x squared.

5
00:00:15,659 --> 00:00:18,530
Every y shows up inside y over x.

6
00:00:18,950 --> 00:00:24,080
Look at its slope field. Along any straight line through the origin, y over x is

7
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:27,518
constant, so every slope on that line is the same.

8
00:00:27,518 --> 00:00:30,731
The picture looks identical if you zoom in or out.

9
00:00:34,831 --> 00:00:39,511
How do you test for it? Lecture 5 uses homogeneous functions.

10
00:00:39,511 --> 00:00:41,819
Replace x by t x and y by t y.

11
00:00:41,819 --> 00:00:47,622
If t to some power comes out in front, the function is homogeneous of that degree.

12
00:00:48,042 --> 00:00:52,559
x cubed plus y cubed gives t cubed in front, so it is degree three.

13
00:00:52,559 --> 00:00:56,961
x squared plus five x y, degree two. But x squared plus y?

14
00:00:56,961 --> 00:01:00,494
The powers do not match, so it is not homogeneous.

15
00:01:00,914 --> 00:01:05,090
For M dx plus N dy equals zero, the rule is simple.

16
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:08,727
M and N must be homogeneous of the same degree.

17
00:01:12,427 --> 00:01:16,386
The fix is one substitution. Let u equal y over x.

18
00:01:16,386 --> 00:01:19,231
In other words, y equals u times x.

19
00:01:19,651 --> 00:01:22,685
Now differentiate with the product rule.

20
00:01:22,685 --> 00:01:25,268
dy/dx equals u, plus x du/dx.

21
00:01:25,268 --> 00:01:28,560
That product rule is the step people forget.

22
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:33,654
Watch it on the prototype. The left side becomes u plus x u prime.

23
00:01:33,654 --> 00:01:36,925
The right side becomes one plus u plus u squared.

24
00:01:37,345 --> 00:01:39,358
The u cancels on both sides.

25
00:01:39,358 --> 00:01:43,499
x u prime equals one plus u squared. And now it separates.

26
00:01:43,919 --> 00:01:48,792
Integrate: arc tangent of u equals natural log of x, plus C.

27
00:01:48,792 --> 00:01:55,224
Then put back u equals y over x, and y equals x tangent of natural log of x, plus C.

28
00:01:59,124 --> 00:02:01,579
Now Example 2 from Lecture 5.

29
00:02:01,579 --> 00:02:08,057
x squared plus y squared, dx, plus x squared minus x y, dy, equals zero.

30
00:02:08,477 --> 00:02:12,669
Both M and N are degree two, so it is homogeneous.

31
00:02:12,669 --> 00:02:16,929
Use y equals u x, so dy equals u dx, plus x du.

32
00:02:17,350 --> 00:02:22,075
Substitute. Every term has an x squared, which is exactly why this works.

33
00:02:22,494 --> 00:02:25,167
Divide by x squared, and collect.

34
00:02:25,167 --> 00:02:28,508
The u squared terms cancel, leaving one plus u,

35
00:02:28,508 --> 00:02:32,456
dx, plus x times one minus u, du, equals zero.

36
00:02:32,876 --> 00:02:36,125
Divide by x times one plus u to separate.

37
00:02:36,125 --> 00:02:41,539
dx over x, plus one minus u over one plus u, du, equals zero.

38
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:46,992
Rewrite that fraction as two over one plus u, minus one, and integrate.

39
00:02:46,992 --> 00:02:52,843
Natural log of x, plus two natural log of one plus u, minus u, equals a constant.

40
00:02:53,263 --> 00:02:55,744
Combine the logs and exponentiate.

41
00:02:55,744 --> 00:02:59,313
x times one plus u squared, equals c e to the u.

42
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:03,365
Finally put back u equals y over x, and clean up.

43
00:03:03,365 --> 00:03:07,481
x plus y, squared, equals c x, e to the y over x.

44
00:03:07,901 --> 00:03:11,905
We divided by one plus u, so check y equals negative x.

45
00:03:11,905 --> 00:03:16,577
It does solve the equation, and it is included when c equals zero.

46
00:03:20,478 --> 00:03:21,361
Three traps.

47
00:03:21,781 --> 00:03:26,394
One. The product rule. dy is u dx plus x du.

48
00:03:26,394 --> 00:03:30,369
Not just x du, and dy/dx is not just u prime.

49
00:03:30,789 --> 00:03:33,518
Two. Not checking the degrees first.

50
00:03:33,518 --> 00:03:37,720
If M and N have different degrees, y equals u x will not separate.

51
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:40,004
Three. Stopping in u.

52
00:03:40,004 --> 00:03:44,053
Always put back u equals y over x, and check what you divided by.

53
00:03:47,852 --> 00:03:49,116
Here is the recipe.

54
00:03:49,536 --> 00:03:51,912
One. Check M and N have the same degree.

55
00:03:52,333 --> 00:03:55,010
Two. Let y equal u x, with the product rule.

56
00:03:55,430 --> 00:03:58,685
Three. Cancel the power of x, separate, and integrate.

57
00:03:59,105 --> 00:04:03,337
Four. Put back u equals y over x, and check what you divided by.

58
00:04:03,757 --> 00:04:08,011
Next up, equations where x and y travel together in one bundle.

59
00:04:08,011 --> 00:04:10,310
The linear argument substitution.
